e sandals of Mercury and the sword which had been given to him in order to complete this task. The statue was carved by Antonio Canova (1757-1822) in the space of a few months between the end of the year 1800 and the beginning of 1801.&lt;\/i&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1460\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Description: Perseo trionfante &lt;i&gt;shows the triumphant Persus holding the severed head of the Medusa, one of the three Gorgons. The hero is shown with the winged cap, the sandals of Mercury and the sword which had been given to him in order to complete this task. The statue was carved by Antonio Canova (1757-1822) in the space of a few months between the end of the year 1800 and the beginning of 1801.&lt;\/i&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_1998\/476923291.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Description: Perseo trionfante <i>shows the triumphant Persus holding the severed head of the Medusa, one of the three Gorgons. The hero is shown with the winged cap, the sandals of Mercury and the sword which had been given to him in order to complete this task. The statue was carved by Antonio Canova (1757-1822) in the space of a few months between the end of the year 1800 and the beginning of 1801.<\/i><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2022.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Gallery of World Maps\" data-attachment-id=\"1465\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Gallery of World Maps\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2022\/891962350.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Gallery of World Maps<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2027.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Sala dell&#039;Immacolata\n&lt;p&gt;Description: &lt;i&gt;Following the proclamation of the dogma of the Immaculate Conception by Pius IX on 8 December 1854 the Pontiff decided to celebrate the event with a cycle of frescoes. The large hall adjacent to the rooms of Raphael in the Borgia Tower was chosen and the work was commissioned from the Ancona artist Francesco Podesti (1800-1895). He worked on the project from 1856 to 1865, designing and completing each part himself.&lt;\/i&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1466\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Sala dell&#039;Immacolata\n&lt;p&gt;Description: &lt;i&gt;Following the proclamation of the dogma of the Immaculate Conception by Pius IX on 8 December 1854 the Pontiff decided to celebrate the event with a cycle of frescoes. The large hall adjacent to the rooms of Raphael in the Borgia Tower was chosen and the work was commissioned from the Ancona artist Francesco Podesti (1800-1895). He worked on the project from 1856 to 1865, designing and completing each part himself.&lt;\/i&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2027\/1222046995.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Sala dell'Immacolata\n<p>Description: <i>Following the proclamation of the dogma of the Immaculate Conception by Pius IX on 8 December 1854 the Pontiff decided to celebrate the event with a cycle of frescoes. The large hall adjacent to the rooms of Raphael in the Borgia Tower was chosen and the work was commissioned from the Ancona artist Francesco Podesti (1800-1895). He worked on the project from 1856 to 1865, designing and completing each part himself.<\/i><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2032.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Description: &lt;i&gt;The Room of Heliodorus is the second in which Raphael worked. It is impressive because of the quality of the painting, the splendid colors, the magnificent unsurpassed effects of light as well as the striking portraits. Some areas in the grotesques and arches of the ceiling are attributed to Luca Signorelli, Bramantino. Lorenxo Lotto and Cesare de Sesto. These form part of the first decoration which Julius II commissioned at the beginning of his papacy. The work was interrupted and then substituted by what we see today due to the pope&#039;s admiration for Raphael&#039;s first frescoes in the adjacent Room of the Signature. Raphael worked in this room from 1511 till 1514, lastly substituting the grotesques at the center of the ceiling with four episodes from the Old Testament: the Pact between God and Noah, the Sacrifice of Isaac, Moses before the Burning Bush and Jacob&#039;s Ladder.&lt;\/i&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"1467\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Description: &lt;i&gt;The Room of Heliodorus is the second in which Raphael worked. It is impressive because of the quality of the painting, the splendid colors, the magnificent unsurpassed effects of light as well as the striking portraits. Some areas in the grotesques and arches of the ceiling are attributed to Luca Signorelli, Bramantino. Lorenxo Lotto and Cesare de Sesto. These form part of the first decoration which Julius II commissioned at the beginning of his papacy. The work was interrupted and then substituted by what we see today due to the pope&#039;s admiration for Raphael&#039;s first frescoes in the adjacent Room of the Signature. Raphael worked in this room from 1511 till 1514, lastly substituting the grotesques at the center of the ceiling with four episodes from the Old Testament: the Pact between God and Noah, the Sacrifice of Isaac, Moses before the Burning Bush and Jacob&#039;s Ladder.&lt;\/i&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2032\/2996209020.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Description: <i>The Room of Heliodorus is the second in which Raphael worked. It is impressive because of the quality of the painting, the splendid colors, the magnificent unsurpassed effects of light as well as the striking portraits. Some areas in the grotesques and arches of the ceiling are attributed to Luca Signorelli, Bramantino. Lorenxo Lotto and Cesare de Sesto. These form part of the first decoration which Julius II commissioned at the beginning of his papacy. The work was interrupted and then substituted by what we see today due to the pope's admiration for Raphael's first frescoes in the adjacent Room of the Signature. Raphael worked in this room from 1511 till 1514, lastly substituting the grotesques at the center of the ceiling with four episodes from the Old Testament: the Pact between God and Noah, the Sacrifice of Isaac, Moses before the Burning Bush and Jacob's Ladder.<\/i><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2040.jpg\" data-attachment-id=\"1468\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2040\/1975301429.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2016.jpg\" data-attachment-id=\"1463\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibaffl